# One Verify site's settings, all in one file: the AWS account and domain,
# the SSH key, the eVerify credentials, Django's secrets and every optional
# feature. deploy/deploy.sh reads it to build the AWS resources (with
# Terraform) and on every deploy, when it writes the server's .env from it.
#
# A site shares nothing with any other deployment of Verify: it has its own
# AWS account, its own network, its own domain and certificate, its own
# eVerify credentials and its own database. Nothing here defaults to
# another site's values, and the tools refuse to use AWS credentials that
# belong to any account but the one named below.
#
# This file holds secrets. Copy it OUT of the repository, into a private
# folder of its own, and keep that folder backed up:
#
#   verify-site/
#     site.conf             this file, filled in
#     keys/verify, .pub     the SSH key (ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f keys/verify)
#     secrets/              issuer and Apple Wallet PEMs, if used
#     terraform.tfstate     written by `deploy.sh infra`; keep it with the rest
#
# Then, from the repository:
#
#   export VERIFY_SITE=../verify-site/site.conf
#   deploy/deploy.sh infra        # build the AWS resources
#   deploy/deploy.sh              # deploy the application
#
# Format: KEY=value, one per line. No quotes, no spaces around "=". A
# comment goes on a line of its own, so a value may contain "#". Relative
# paths are relative to this file. An unknown key is refused rather than
# ignored, so a typo cannot quietly fall back to a default. Leave a key
# empty, or commented out, to keep the default shown.

# =============================================================================
# The site
# =============================================================================

# Short name, unique in the AWS account: lower-case letters, digits and
# hyphens. Names every AWS resource. Choose it once: renaming rebuilds.
SITE_NAME=verify

# The public host name operators will use.
DOMAIN=verify.example.org

# =============================================================================
# AWS: an account of the site's own
# =============================================================================
# Use an AWS account that holds nothing else, so this site cannot touch, or
# be touched by, anything another deployment runs. Every AWS action is
# checked against AWS_ACCOUNT_ID first, and Terraform itself refuses any
# other account.
AWS_ACCOUNT_ID=
AWS_REGION=

# The site's credentials, of an IAM user (or role) in that account allowed to
# build what docs/deploy-aws.md lists. Either access keys here, which keeps
# everything in this one file...
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# ...or the name of a profile in the AWS CLI's configuration on this machine,
# such as one signed in through IAM Identity Center. Not both. With neither,
# the tools stop: they never fall back to this machine's default
# credentials, which may belong to another account.
# AWS_PROFILE=

# Who may reach the bastion over SSH: comma-separated CIDR ranges, such as
# your office's public address as a /32.
SSH_ALLOWED_CIDRS=203.0.113.10/32

# The SSH key deploys use. The public half is installed on the servers.
SSH_PUBLIC_KEY_FILE=keys/verify.pub
SSH_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=keys/verify

# The site's TLS certificate. With a Route 53 hosted zone for the domain,
# validation and the site's DNS record are automatic. Without one,
# `deploy.sh infra certificate` prints the record to create. Or name an
# issued ACM certificate you already have, in this region.
# ROUTE53_ZONE_ID=
# CERTIFICATE_ARN=

# INSTANCE_TYPE=t3.micro
# BASTION_INSTANCE_TYPE=t3.nano
# VPC_CIDR=10.20.0.0/16
# ROOT_VOLUME_GB=30
# SNAPSHOT_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# =============================================================================
# Django
# =============================================================================

# A long random string, unique to this site. Generate one with
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(64))"
# The site refuses to start without it.
DJANGO_SECRET_KEY=

# Keys the hash User Records finds a person's transactions by. Blank uses
# DJANGO_SECRET_KEY. Changing it later orphans every key already written, so
# set it once, before DJANGO_SECRET_KEY is ever rotated. Generate one with
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(64))"
# USER_RECORDS_KEY=

# The first superuser, for the Django admin at /admin/. Created on the
# server's first deploy only; empty the password once the account exists.
DJANGO_SUPERUSER_USERNAME=admin
DJANGO_SUPERUSER_EMAIL=admin@example.org
DJANGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=

# Defaults to DOMAIN; the deploy also adds the server's private address,
# where the load balancer's health checks arrive. Never True on a server.
# DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=
# DJANGO_DEBUG=False

# =============================================================================
# eVerify: the client that signs operators in
# =============================================================================
# Issued by PSA to THIS site. Never reuse another deployment's client: its
# usage would be billed to, and logged against, somebody else. These apply until a site administrator saves a client on the
# Setup page, which then takes precedence. Each organization's VERIFICATION
# client is entered on that organization's own page in the application (its
# address by a site administrator, on the Site page), and deliberately never
# falls back to these.
EVERIFY_CLIENT_ID=
EVERIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=
EVERIFY_PUBLIC_API_KEY=
# EVERIFY_BASE_URL=https://ws.everify.gov.ph/api
# The only hosts any eVerify address may name, comma-separated. Whatever
# answers there decides who a verified person is, so no page can widen this.
# Change it only to point a test site at a stub.
# EVERIFY_ALLOWED_HOSTS=ws.everify.gov.ph
# The hosts eVerify's portrait links may name, comma-separated. Empty allows
# any host that resolves only to public addresses; loopback, the private
# network and the cloud metadata service are refused whatever this says. The
# first fetch from each host is logged: name it here to allow only that.
# EVERIFY_FACE_HOSTS=

# The single sign-on issuers Setup may choose, exactly as each provider names
# itself, comma-separated. With SSO selected the issuer decides who signs in,
# so no page can widen this. Empty by default: name your provider here before
# selecting SSO on Setup.
# SSO_ALLOWED_ISSUERS=https://id.example.org/oidc

# How many proxies append to X-Forwarded-For in front of the application: the
# load balancer and nginx, so 2. The client's address, which the per-address
# limits count, is the entry this many from the end.
# VERIFY_PROXY_HOPS=2

# However busy it is, a sign-in ends this many seconds after it was made.
# SESSION_ABSOLUTE_SECONDS=43200

# Browsers that have been here once refuse plain http for this host for this
# many seconds. Include subdomains only if you own every one of them.
# VERIFY_HSTS_SECONDS=31536000
# VERIFY_HSTS_INCLUDE_SUBDOMAINS=False

# =============================================================================
# Optional: offline credentials in the document QR
# =============================================================================
# Without an issuer key the document still prints, with a QR that holds only
# the transaction id. The key and the epoch policy must match the
# OfflineVerifier deployment whose phones will read the credentials:
# `manage.py load_credential_keys` copies both from one and refuses on a
# mismatch.
#
# ISSUER_KEY_FILE is the PEM on THIS machine. The deploy copies it to the
# server's secrets directory and points CREDENTIAL_ISSUER_KEY_PATH at it.
# ISSUER_KEY_FILE=secrets/verify-issuer.pem
# CREDENTIAL_ISSUER_KEY_PATH=
# CREDENTIAL_ISSUER_KEY_ID=1
# Back on the key file after a credential signer: nothing is minted unless
# this names CREDENTIAL_ISSUER_KEY_ID, the operator's word that the signer's
# last deployment key was that same key (signer/deploy/README.md, "Leaving
# signer mode").
# CREDENTIAL_FILE_MODE_AFTER_SIGNER=
# Wraps the epoch private keys at rest: 64 hex characters, or base64. Losing
# it orphans every stored epoch key. Generate one with
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"
# CREDENTIAL_EPOCH_WRAP_KEY=
# CREDENTIAL_GENESIS=1750000000
# CREDENTIAL_EPOCH_SECONDS=2592000
# CREDENTIAL_OVERLAP_EPOCHS=3
# CREDENTIAL_MAX_VALIDITY_DAYS=365
# The most QR codes one document prints: credential, portrait and attachment
# parts together; 0 is no cap, and 1 is refused (a photo needs two).
# CREDENTIAL_MAX_QR_CODES=2
# CREDENTIAL_REVOCATION_TTL_SECONDS=86400
# CREDENTIAL_REVOCATION_REBUILD_SECONDS=300
#
# A credential signer (signer/deploy/README.md) holds the issuer keys
# instead of the PEM file: its app socket, the issuing CAs' fingerprints the
# phones pin, and the SHA-256 of the token its reconciler presents.
# SIGNER_SOCKET=/run/cl03-signer/app.sock
# CREDENTIAL_ISSUER_CA_FINGERPRINTS=
# SIGNER_REPORT_TOKEN_SHA256=
# Or a signer on a host of its own that dials out to this site and polls it
# for jobs (signer/deploy/README.md, "Poll mode"): SIGNER_SOCKET empty, and
# the key both ends sign with, 64 hex characters, the same as the signer's
# credential poll-key. That key is on every app server, so anyone with a copy
# can act as the signer from any address the proxy admits: the site's proxy
# must admit /api/v1/signer/jobs/, answers/ and report/ only from the
# signer's own egress addresses, and route them to a gunicorn pool of their
# own. This kit's nginx answers 404 for /api/v1/signer/. A request waits at
# most SIGNER_QUEUE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS for the signer's answer.
# SIGNER_QUEUE_KEY=
# SIGNER_QUEUE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# How many of a worker's threads may wait for the signer at once, below
# gunicorn's threads a worker: by default one fewer than GUNICORN_THREADS,
# or 1. A backstop only: the signer's own requests need a pool of their own
# (README). GUNICORN_THREADS also sets this kit's gunicorn threads (2 unset).
# SIGNER_QUEUE_MAX_WAITING=
# GUNICORN_THREADS=

# =============================================================================
# Optional: verifier phones and the holder's card
# =============================================================================
# Where the pairing QR sends a phone. Empty means the host that served the
# page, which is right behind the load balancer.
# DEVICE_PAIRING_BASE_URL=https://verify.example.org
# DEVICE_PAIRING_TTL_SECONDS=900
# DEVICE_LEASE_SECONDS=2592000
# DEVICE_CHALLENGE_TTL_SECONDS=120
# App Attest: every pairing and sync is checked and recorded; True refuses
# anything that does not pass. The app id is "<TeamID>.<BundleID>"; the
# environment is production for store builds, development for a developer's.
# DEVICE_REQUIRE_APP_ATTEST=False
# DEVICE_APP_ATTEST_APP_ID=ABCDE12345.org.example.verifier
# DEVICE_APP_ATTEST_ENVIRONMENT=production
# Refuse phones that sign /keys/ the old way, once none is left.
# DEVICE_REQUIRE_KEYS_CONTEXT=False
# Activity-log records one phone may store in any 24 hours.
# DEVICE_LOG_DAILY_RECORDS=20000
# Days the activity log is kept, from when a record arrived; a daily timer
# deletes what is older.
# DEVICE_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=360
# CARD_HANDOFF_TTL_SECONDS=300
# The encoder for the face in the portrait symbol: webp, or avif once every
# verifier app and idmanager reading your documents understands it.
# PORTRAIT_FORMAT=webp

# =============================================================================
# Optional: holders registered from idmanager
# =============================================================================
# A person who verified themselves in idmanager registers their own phone and
# is issued a credential. OFF unless both of the first two are set; the phone
# is capped at the public tier and the person cannot sign in here. idmanager
# prints both lines with `manage.py verify_holder_key`.
# HOLDER_ASSERTION_ISSUER=https://idmanager.example.org
# HOLDER_ASSERTION_KEYS=
# HOLDER_ASSERTION_MAX_LIFETIME_SECONDS=600
# HOLDER_VERIFICATION_MAX_AGE_SECONDS=900
# HOLDER_CREDENTIAL_ISSUER_NAME=Self-verified with idmanager
# Refuse holder phones that sign the old way, once none is left; with
# DEVICE_REQUIRE_KEYS_CONTEXT.
# HOLDER_REQUIRE_CONTEXT=False

# =============================================================================
# Optional: Apple Wallet passes
# =============================================================================
# All or none: without them the "Add to Apple Wallet" button is not drawn.
# The three files are PEMs issued against your own Pass Type ID, on THIS
# machine; the deploy copies them to the server and sets the matching
# WALLET_*_PATH. An expired certificate still builds passes, and iOS refuses
# them without a message, so note its expiry date.
# WALLET_PASS_TYPE_ID=pass.org.example.verify
# WALLET_TEAM_ID=
# WALLET_CERT_FILE=secrets/wallet-cert.pem
# WALLET_KEY_FILE=secrets/wallet-key.pem
# WALLET_WWDR_FILE=secrets/wallet-wwdr.pem
# WALLET_CERT_PATH=
# WALLET_KEY_PATH=
# WALLET_WWDR_PATH=

# =============================================================================
# Branding
# =============================================================================
# The site carries the name and logos you give it, and no one else's. With
# none of these set, pages are titled "Verify" and have no footer.

# The name before the colon in every page title.
# SITE_TITLE=Verify

# Logos in the footer of every page. Put the image files (PNG, JPEG, GIF or
# WebP; not SVG) in a folder beside this file and list them, separated by
# ";", each as file|link|alt text. The link and the text are optional. The
# deploy copies the folder to the server and sets BRANDING_DIR to it.
# BRANDING_FOLDER=branding
# SITE_FOOTER_LOGOS=our-logo.png|https://www.example.org|Our organization;partner.png|https://partner.example.org|Our partner
# A line above the logos, such as "Operated by".
# SITE_FOOTER_LABEL=
# BRANDING_DIR=

# =============================================================================
# Rarely needed
# =============================================================================
# Moves an existing pre-organization database's people and data into one
# organization of this name, on that migrate. A new site ignores it.
# VERIFY_DEFAULT_ORG_NAME=PSA

# Development machines only, NEVER a server. With DJANGO_DEBUG=True the first
# replaces eVerify with a mock that signs in whoever the browser names, and
# the deploy refuses it; the second keeps whole eVerify responses (names, ID
# numbers, faces) in the database, with DJANGO_DEBUG=True or with
# VERIFY_WORKBENCH naming one of the site's own hosts -- a development
# server that should not run with Django's debug pages.
# EVERIFY_ALLOW_MOCK_LOGIN=False
# EVERIFY_DEBUG_CAPTURE=False
# VERIFY_WORKBENCH=

# =============================================================================
# A server built some other way (skips Terraform)
# =============================================================================
# By default the deploy finds the server and the bastion in the Terraform
# state beside this file. Set these to deploy to a server you built yourself.
# JUMP_HOST may stay empty for a server reachable directly.
# SERVER_IP=
# JUMP_HOST=
# REMOTE_USER=ec2-user
# APP_DIR=/home/ec2-user/app
# REMOTE_SECRETS_DIR=/home/ec2-user/secrets
